UID:8ff80e55d00a6015@role-family-law.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260807T001345Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260806
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] State of Maine v. Nathan Lee
DESCRIPTION:MAINE SUPREME JUDICIAL COURT Reporter of Decisions \nDecision:
   2026 ME 82 \nDocket: Lin-25-49 \nArgued: November 14\, 2025 \nDecided:  
 August 6\, 2026 \n \nPanel:  STANFILL\, C.J.\, and MEAD\, CONNORS\, LAWREN
 CE\, DOUGLAS\, and LIPEZ\, JJ. \n \n \nSTATE OF MAINE \n \nv. \n \nNATHAN 
 LEE \n \n \nLAWRENCE\, J. \n[¶1]  Nathan Lee appeals from a judgment of c
 onviction on one count of \ndomestic violence aggravated assault (Class B)
  and one count of domestic \nviolence criminal threatening with a dangerou
 s weapon (Class C) entered by \nthe trial court (Lincoln County\, Billings
 \, J.) after a jury trial.  Lee argues that we \nmust vacate his convictio
 n because of two instances of prosecutorial error  \nduring the trial.  Fi
 rst\, Lee contends that the State’s attempt to lay a foundation \nfor a 
 law enforcement witness’s testimony related to Lee’s credibility was \
 nimproper and prejudicial.   Second\, he argues that the State impermissib
 ly  \ncommented on the credibility of a witness during closing arguments. 
  Although \nboth instances were plain errors\, neither error affected Lee
 ’s substantial rights \nnor deprived him of a fair trial.  We therefore 
 affirm the judgment of conviction.  \n 2 \nI.  BACKGROUND \n [¶2]  When v
 iewing the evidence in the light most favorable to the verdict\, \nthe jur
 y could have rationally found the following facts beyond a reasonable \ndo
 ubt.  State v. Gervais\, 2025 ME 27\, ¶ 2\, 334 A.3d 645.   \n [¶3]  Lee
 ’s convictions were based on two separate incidents\, both \ninvolving t
 he same victim\, his daughter\, and both of which occurred at the \nfamily
 ’s home with no other witnesses present.   \n [¶4]  In the spring of 20
 18\, Lee and the victim  came back to their home \nafter a sports practice
  and got into a heated argument.  While the two were \narguing\, Lee went 
 into his room and came back out with his gun.  Lee proceeded \nto point th
 e gun at the victim and said if the victim really did not care\, he could 
 \nend it all here and now.  With the gun still pointed at the victim\, Lee
  clicked the \ntrigger an\nhttps://www.courts.maine.gov/courts/sjc/lawcour
 t/2026/26me082.pdf
URL:https://www.courts.maine.gov/courts/sjc/lawcourt/2026/26me082.pdf
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:3b2b3a46a9c8e19a@role-family-law.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260805T085447Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260804
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] US Department of Labor finds child labor and 
 wage violations  at 3 San Antonio bakery and café locations
DESCRIPTION:SAN ANTONIO – The U.S. Department of Labor has recovered th
 ousands in back wages and assessed penalties after a federal investigation
  revealed child labor\, minimum wage\, and overtime violations by the oper
 ators of three La Panaderia Bakery & Café locations in San Antonio.The de
 partment’s Wage and Hour Division investigated the pay practices of Tequ
 ila Almond Croissant LLC\, Pan Dulce LLC\, and SA Bakery Co. LLC. All thre
 e do business as La Panaderia Bakery & Café in San Antonio with locations
  at 301 E. Houston Street\; 17030 Fiesta Texas Drive\; and 8305 Broadway.D
 ivision investigators found the employers violated child labor provisions 
 of the Fair Labor Standards Act. Specifically\, they employed a 13-year-ol
 d minor who was under the legal age for employment and permitted a 15-year
 -old minor to work overnight\, which is prohibited for 14- and 15-year-old
 s. The division assessed a $25\,706 civil money penalty for the child labo
 r violations.The division’s investigation also revealed minimum wage vio
 lations due to the employers’ failure to pay one worker for two overnigh
 t shifts at the La Cantera café location on Fiesta Texas Drive. The emplo
 yers also failed to combine hours for employees who worked at multiple caf
 é locations and paid the employees straight-time rates instead of the req
 uired time-and-one-half overtime rates. Employers and workers can contact
  the division with questions and requests for compliance assistance at it
 s toll-free helpline\, 866-4US-WAGE (487-9243). Employers are also encour
 aged to use the agency’s industry-specific compliance assistance toolkit
 s to learn about their responsibilities under the laws enforced by the div
 ision. The agency’s PAID program offers employers an opportunity to self
 -report and resolve potential minimum wage and overtime violations under t
 he FLSA\, as well as certain potential violations under the Family and Med
 ical Leave Act.Download the agency’s free timesheet app for iOS and An
 droid devices to track hours and pay.\nhttp://www.dol.gov/newsroom/release
 s/whd/whd20260804
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/whd/whd20260804
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:f32ca95fa3d7341c@role-family-law.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260804T195436Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260804
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Kathleen M. Barron v. John D. Barron
DESCRIPTION:MAINE SUPREME JUDICIAL COURT Reporter of Decisions \nDecision:
  2026 ME 77 \nDocket: Pen-25-563 \nSubmitted \nOn Briefs: June 16\, 2026 \
 nDecided: August 4\, 2026 \n \nPanel: STANFILL\, C.J.\, and LAWRENCE\, DOU
 GLAS\, and TAUB\, JJ.\, and HORTON\, A.R.J.  \n \n \nKATHLEEN M. BARRON \n
  \nv. \n \nJOHN D. BARRON \n \n \nTAUB\, J. \n[¶1]  John D. Barron appeal
 s from a divorce judgment issued by the \nDistrict Court (Bangor\, Roberts
 \, A.R.J. ) dissolving his marriage to Kathleen M. \nBarron.  John contend
 s that the court erred in distributing marital property \nbecause it used 
 information from Kathleen’s 2022 financial statement instead \nof inform
 ation from her 2024 financial statement.\n1  We agree\, vacate the \njudgm
 ent in part\, and remand for further proceedings.  We also deny Kathleen
 ’s \nmotion for sanctions\, which argues that John’s appeal is frivolo
 us.   \n \n1  Because the parties share the same surname\, we refer to the
 m by their given names.   \n 2 \nI.  BACKGROUND \n[¶2]  Kathleen filed fo
 r divorce from John in 2022.  In 2022\, both Kathleen \nand John filed fin
 ancial statements as required by M.R. Civ.  P. 108(c).  On \nMay 15\, 2024
 \, prior to the final hearing on May  31\, 2024\, Kathleen filed an \nupda
 ted financial statement as required by M.R. Civ. P. 108(d)(4).  John did n
 ot \nfile an updated financial statement. \n[¶3]  At the final hearing\, 
 the only issue s were spousal support and the \ndivision of marital proper
 ty.  Kathleen entered her 2024 financial statement in \nevidence and testi
 fied as to its contents.  Kathleen also entered John’s \n2022 financial 
 statement in evidence.   Neither Kathleen’s 2022 financial \nstatement n
 or an updated financial statement from John were entered in \nevidence.   
 \n[¶4]  After the hearing\, the parties submitted proposed calculations f
 or \nthe division of their marital property.  In her proposed calculation\
 , Kathleen did \nnot use the information from her testimony or her 2024 fi
 nancial statement.  \nInstead\, she used both her and John’s 2022 financ
 ial statements and calculat\nhttps://www.courts.maine.gov/courts/sjc/lawco
 urt/2026/26me077.pdf
URL:https://www.courts.maine.gov/courts/sjc/lawcourt/2026/26me077.pdf
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
